WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.760
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.060
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.340
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.600
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:19.400
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.560
We're The Office ladies.

8
00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:26.930
Okay, hi.

9
00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:30.450
It's Ed, hi.

10
00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:31.710
Surprise, you guys.

11
00:00:31.870 --> 00:00:36.430
We have Ed Helms here today, and we will be talking about Andy's play.

12
00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:41.730
I didn't know if I was supposed to talk right away or if you were going to introduce me, but then you both looked at me.

13
00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:43.730
We just stared at you until you spoke.

14
00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:45.250
Okay, so yeah, I'm talking.

15
00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:45.870
Now I'm talking.

16
00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:46.390
I'm here.

17
00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:50.570
Well, this is Season 7, Episode 3.

18
00:00:50.770 --> 00:00:54.490
It was written by Charlie Grandy, directed by John Scott.

19
00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:55.810
Would you like a summary?

20
00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:57.510
I mean, I would love that one more.

21
00:00:57.630 --> 00:00:58.790
Ed, what do you think about a summary?

22
00:00:58.890 --> 00:00:59.250
Feeling good?

23
00:00:59.310 --> 00:00:59.870
I love summaries.

24
00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:00.610
I love a summary.

25
00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:01.370
Here it is.

26
00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:10.850
Andy invites his co-workers to watch him in a community theater production of Sweeney Todd, which Michael is upset to learn he was not cast in.

27
00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:16.790
The entire office attends, except for Aaron, who steps in at the last minute to babysit for Cece.

28
00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:25.910
And Angela is going to get another punch on her punch card from Dwight, but not for the reason, you might think.

29
00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:27.390
There it is.

30
00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:27.950
What do we think?

31
00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:29.830
I thought that was a fantastic summary.

32
00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:30.710
It's actually, yeah.

33
00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:37.650
I re-watched the episode in preparation for this, and that summary is spot on.

34
00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:39.250
Oh, thank you.

35
00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:39.790
Yeah.

36
00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:41.730
I wrote it myself.

37
00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:51.050
I also thought maybe we want a quick summary of Sweeney Todd, the production that Andy is starring in, to give some context.

38
00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:52.070
What do you guys think?

39
00:01:52.390 --> 00:01:53.890
I want to hear it.

40
00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:57.470
I went down the Wikipedia rabbit hole about Sweeney Todd, and wow.

41
00:01:57.850 --> 00:01:58.210
Oh.

42
00:01:58.650 --> 00:01:59.890
Well, I hope I cover it.

43
00:01:59.930 --> 00:02:01.290
If I don't, will you let me know?

44
00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:01.890
Maybe.

45
00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:02.830
Oh, no.

46
00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:04.490
I didn't commit any of it to memory.

47
00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:05.630
There's just a lot of versions.

48
00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:12.370
I don't know anything about it, so I won't know if – I mean, I know what is in the episode, basically.

49
00:02:12.710 --> 00:02:13.070
Okay.

50
00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:14.050
But I don't know the –

51
00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:14.530
The musical.

52
00:02:14.570 --> 00:02:19.510
And I know the premise, but I don't know the story, so anything you say is going to be fine with me.

53
00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:20.190
All right.

54
00:02:20.330 --> 00:02:20.990
Well, here we go.

55
00:02:20.990 --> 00:02:28.050
So, Sweeney Todd, The Demon Barber of Fleet Street is a real musical that first opened on Broadway in 1979.

56
00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:32.930
The music and lyrics are by Stephen Sondheim, and the book is by Hugh Wheeler.

57
00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:43.530
The character of Sweeney Todd, a murderous barber who goes around killing people, it originated in Victorian serialized fiction known as The Penny Dreadfuls.

58
00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:43.930
Yes.

59
00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:45.850
I saw that on Wikipedia.

60
00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:48.710
I have one little beef with your summary, actually.

61
00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:49.230
Are you done?

62
00:02:49.270 --> 00:02:49.690
You're not done.

63
00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:51.150
I have a little more to say.

64
00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:51.370
Okay.

65
00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:51.830
Okay, go for it.

66
00:02:51.910 --> 00:02:52.190
Okay.

67
00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:52.610
Sorry.

68
00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:55.510
Now, already one beef I've earned.

69
00:02:55.730 --> 00:02:55.950
No.

70
00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:56.610
All right.

71
00:02:56.770 --> 00:03:04.650
The musical was based on a play by Christopher Bond where he introduced a psychological backstory for Todd's crimes.

72
00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:10.330
And Stephen Sondheim had seen this play, and that inspired him to write the musical.

73
00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:17.710
Over 80% of Sweeney Todd is set to music, either through singing or an underscore to dialogue.

74
00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:23.770
And the musical won both the Tony and the Olivier Award for Best New Musical when it came out.

75
00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:25.630
When was that that it came out?

76
00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:26.610
1979.

77
00:03:27.050 --> 00:03:27.570
Oh, right.

78
00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:28.350
What's your beef, Ed?

79
00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:31.370
Okay, so you said that he goes around killing people.

80
00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:35.550
He actually is a barber, and he murders people in his barbershop.

81
00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:39.410
So, really, people come around to be murdered.

82
00:03:39.530 --> 00:03:39.970
Exactly.

83
00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:40.310
Yes.

84
00:03:40.390 --> 00:03:41.510
That would have been more accurate.

85
00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:42.990
He doesn't go around murdering.

86
00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:45.430
People come around to get murdered.

87
00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:46.790
More accurate.

88
00:03:46.990 --> 00:03:48.030
They come around for a haircut.

89
00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:49.570
They come around for a haircut, yeah.

90
00:03:49.570 --> 00:03:51.270
And then he kills them with the straight razor.

91
00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:51.570
Right.

92
00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:53.050
And then he opens the chair.

93
00:03:53.050 --> 00:03:56.290
He has a lever, and they go flying down into the basement.

94
00:03:56.610 --> 00:04:02.250
And then he has a friend who turns their bodies into meat for really bad mince pies.

95
00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:03.850
Aren't they really yummy?

96
00:04:04.270 --> 00:04:04.830
Oh, yeah.

97
00:04:04.910 --> 00:04:05.130
Sorry.

98
00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:07.710
She has a song at the beginning that she has really bad pies.

99
00:04:07.930 --> 00:04:08.110
Oh.

100
00:04:08.410 --> 00:04:10.430
When the pies are people, then they're good?

101
00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:12.290
Yeah, then people can't get enough of the pies.

102
00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:13.030
Oh, my God.

103
00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:15.250
I did not realize this part of the story.

104
00:04:15.350 --> 00:04:15.810
Oh, yeah.

105
00:04:16.030 --> 00:04:16.510
Oh, yeah.

106
00:04:16.630 --> 00:04:17.650
There's a whole cannibalism.

107
00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:19.490
I mean, it's like accidental cannibalism.

108
00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:21.510
They sell them as mince pies.

109
00:04:21.610 --> 00:04:21.910
Yeah.

110
00:04:21.990 --> 00:04:22.850
But they're people.

111
00:04:22.850 --> 00:04:23.790
It's like Soylent Green.

112
00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:24.270
Yeah.

113
00:04:24.390 --> 00:04:24.730
It's people.

114
00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:25.090
Yeah.

115
00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:26.130
It's people, people.

116
00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:26.770
It's people.

117
00:04:26.830 --> 00:04:27.370
Come on, people.

118
00:04:27.830 --> 00:04:28.190
Yeah.

119
00:04:28.390 --> 00:04:28.950
People pies.

120
00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:29.930
People pies.

121
00:04:29.950 --> 00:04:30.050
Wow.

122
00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:32.210
The musical could have been called People Pies.

123
00:04:32.390 --> 00:04:32.990
It should have been.

124
00:04:33.130 --> 00:04:33.870
It should have been, really.

125
00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:37.210
I'm going to, yeah.

126
00:04:37.330 --> 00:04:39.170
We've got to write Stephen Sondheim a letter.

127
00:04:39.350 --> 00:04:39.510
Yeah.

128
00:04:39.510 --> 00:04:41.870
I'm getting out my old timey type of letter.

129
00:04:43.330 --> 00:04:47.130
Well, Ed, we can't wait to talk to you about this episode and your experience.

130
00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:51.730
We thought we'd start by asking you, do you have any musical theater background?

131
00:04:52.070 --> 00:04:52.350
Yeah.

132
00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:53.490
Have you ever been in musicals?

133
00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:54.270
Like in high school?

134
00:04:54.510 --> 00:04:55.170
Well, yeah.

135
00:04:55.170 --> 00:04:59.910
So I was in some musical theater productions in high school.

136
00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:05.390
And then I was in, let's see, I was in Joseph and the Amazing Technicolor Dreamcoat.

137
00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:05.930
Okay.

138
00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:07.250
Oh, Jenna and I both have one.

139
00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:08.550
And we're wondering if you were in.

140
00:05:08.650 --> 00:05:09.030
Keep going.

141
00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:11.870
And gosh, I'm drawing a blank.

142
00:05:11.990 --> 00:05:13.790
Oh, I was in Into the Woods, another Sondheim.

143
00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:14.430
Oh, yes.

144
00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:14.930
Into the Woods.

145
00:05:14.990 --> 00:05:16.410
Another Sondheim masterpiece.

146
00:05:17.890 --> 00:05:19.190
And that was all high school.

147
00:05:19.350 --> 00:05:25.970
And then when I was in college, I did, I was in a musical called The Cradle Will Rock,

148
00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:33.590
which is a really cool, like during the Great Depression, it was written as like a sort of pro worker.

149
00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:35.550
Anyway, it was a cool piece.

150
00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:37.470
And that's what I did in college.

151
00:05:37.590 --> 00:05:38.410
And then I think that's it.

152
00:05:39.190 --> 00:05:39.330
Wow.

153
00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:40.770
No Guys and Dolls, Jenna.

154
00:05:40.910 --> 00:05:42.610
We really were positive.

155
00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:44.890
Really feeling like that'd be a good role for you.

156
00:05:45.250 --> 00:05:46.050
Nathan Detroit.

157
00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:46.410
Yeah.

158
00:05:46.510 --> 00:05:46.630
Sure.

159
00:05:46.870 --> 00:05:47.430
Guys and Dolls.

160
00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:48.370
Sit down, sit down, sit down, sit down.

161
00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:48.990
Yeah.

162
00:05:49.030 --> 00:05:49.570
Sit down, yeah, right.

163
00:05:50.290 --> 00:05:54.010
I don't, I'm not that, I don't know Guys and Dolls.

164
00:05:54.630 --> 00:05:57.850
And I wasn't in it, but I wish I was.

165
00:05:58.090 --> 00:05:59.630
Because then we'd have a lot more to talk about.

166
00:06:02.290 --> 00:06:03.950
Did you do musical theater, Angela?

167
00:06:03.950 --> 00:06:09.010
Oh, I mean, I was in The Music Man at my school in Indonesia.

168
00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:09.610
Awesome.

169
00:06:09.810 --> 00:06:10.050
Mm-hmm.

170
00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:11.670
I played Amaryllis.

171
00:06:12.250 --> 00:06:16.710
And we thought it was really funny we would give everyone a nickname based off their character names.

172
00:06:16.730 --> 00:06:18.790
So everyone called me Amaryllis.

173
00:06:19.130 --> 00:06:19.550
Oh.

174
00:06:19.810 --> 00:06:19.990
Yeah.

175
00:06:19.990 --> 00:06:24.470
So you, your life has just been a series of nicknames.

176
00:06:24.690 --> 00:06:26.090
People love nicknaming you.

177
00:06:26.270 --> 00:06:26.330
I know.

178
00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:27.570
Hambone.

179
00:06:28.270 --> 00:06:29.390
I'm a real ass.

180
00:06:30.310 --> 00:06:32.610
That was my big musical theater debut.

181
00:06:32.730 --> 00:06:35.170
But I actually get really anxious singing in front of people.

182
00:06:35.770 --> 00:06:40.350
I can be on stage doing, you know, zany comedy skits and things like that.

183
00:06:40.490 --> 00:06:42.430
But to sing in front of people makes me so anxious.

184
00:06:42.430 --> 00:06:46.930
So I opted then to go into the more serious theater productions.

185
00:06:47.390 --> 00:06:48.190
Of course.

186
00:06:48.430 --> 00:06:48.910
I see.

187
00:06:49.410 --> 00:06:50.250
What about you?

188
00:06:50.890 --> 00:06:52.890
I did in high school.

189
00:06:53.670 --> 00:06:55.390
I'm not a strong singer.

190
00:06:55.450 --> 00:06:57.270
So I was in the dance chorus.

191
00:06:57.550 --> 00:06:58.410
I've heard you sing.

192
00:06:58.550 --> 00:06:59.610
I think you have a great voice.

193
00:06:59.890 --> 00:07:00.950
Thank you for saying that.

194
00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:02.370
I'm really self-conscious about it.

195
00:07:02.470 --> 00:07:02.630
Oh.

196
00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:04.190
Well, I think.

197
00:07:04.330 --> 00:07:05.750
Anyway, I think you have a great voice.

198
00:07:05.890 --> 00:07:06.550
So you danced.

199
00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:07.290
You were in the chorus.

200
00:07:07.290 --> 00:07:08.530
I did the dancing chorus.

201
00:07:08.530 --> 00:07:13.930
And then my senior year, I got the lead dance role in Fiddler on the Roof.

202
00:07:13.950 --> 00:07:14.830
I was the Fiddler.

203
00:07:14.970 --> 00:07:15.310
Wow.

204
00:07:16.230 --> 00:07:19.150
To which I say, without me, it was just called The Roof.

205
00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:19.870
Wow.

206
00:07:19.990 --> 00:07:20.310
Yeah, of course.

207
00:07:20.310 --> 00:07:21.550
You really, it's an important role.

208
00:07:21.630 --> 00:07:23.250
I had no lines and didn't sing.

209
00:07:23.410 --> 00:07:25.850
But I danced my heart out and I pretended to fiddle.

210
00:07:26.030 --> 00:07:26.350
Yeah.

211
00:07:26.950 --> 00:07:27.590
That's awesome.

212
00:07:27.690 --> 00:07:29.450
I just remembered another one that I was in.

213
00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:29.870
What?

214
00:07:30.390 --> 00:07:30.790
Godspell.

215
00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:33.110
Oh, I was in Godspell in college.

216
00:07:33.230 --> 00:07:33.550
Hey.

217
00:07:33.810 --> 00:07:34.250
High five.

218
00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:34.830
High five.

219
00:07:35.110 --> 00:07:35.370
Yeah.

220
00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:38.070
And then, oh, and in college, I was in.

221
00:07:38.450 --> 00:07:39.230
It's all dancing.

222
00:07:39.390 --> 00:07:39.950
Bob Fosse.

223
00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:41.070
Oh, Chicago?

224
00:07:41.350 --> 00:07:41.510
Oh, Horse Line?

225
00:07:41.990 --> 00:07:42.430
No.

226
00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:44.090
The one.

227
00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:47.010
Nice job, us just pulling out some Fosse.

228
00:07:47.330 --> 00:07:48.130
Liza Minnelli.

229
00:07:50.250 --> 00:07:50.690
Cabaret?

230
00:07:51.010 --> 00:07:51.550
That's it.

231
00:07:51.630 --> 00:07:52.110
Thank you, Devin.

232
00:07:52.110 --> 00:07:52.570
Thank you, Devin.

233
00:07:52.750 --> 00:07:53.670
I was going to say Arthur.

234
00:07:53.890 --> 00:07:54.150
No.

235
00:07:55.970 --> 00:07:56.410
Yes.

236
00:07:56.410 --> 00:07:59.490
And also in college, I was in cabaret and I got to choreograph that.

237
00:07:59.730 --> 00:08:02.770
So very like dance forward musicals were my thing.

238
00:08:03.110 --> 00:08:04.210
I'm very impressed.

239
00:08:04.390 --> 00:08:04.870
Thank you.

240
00:08:05.370 --> 00:08:05.690
Okay.

241
00:08:05.890 --> 00:08:09.150
So we are here to talk about Andy performing Sweeney Todd.

242
00:08:10.090 --> 00:08:10.890
Randy Cordray.

243
00:08:11.110 --> 00:08:12.630
We talk to Randy Cordray all the time.

244
00:08:12.770 --> 00:08:13.290
He's amazing.

245
00:08:13.610 --> 00:08:18.630
He told us the writers originally pitched the storyline over the summer between seasons

246
00:08:18.630 --> 00:08:19.610
six and seven.

247
00:08:20.210 --> 00:08:21.570
How did you first hear about it?

248
00:08:21.710 --> 00:08:24.490
Did they come to you and say, hey, Ed, we want to do a full musical.

249
00:08:24.690 --> 00:08:25.710
You're going to sing a bunch.

250
00:08:25.910 --> 00:08:26.750
Like, did you have any input?

251
00:08:27.290 --> 00:08:31.530
I don't recall knowing anything about it before the table read.

252
00:08:31.530 --> 00:08:33.910
I'm shocked and also not shocked.

253
00:08:34.050 --> 00:08:34.130
Not shocked.

254
00:08:34.130 --> 00:08:34.230
Yeah.

255
00:08:34.310 --> 00:08:38.130
I mean, we were so buried in whatever episode we were working on.

256
00:08:38.250 --> 00:08:40.530
It's not like there was a lot of extra time.

257
00:08:40.690 --> 00:08:47.350
But now that you mention it, I'm sure that I must have started rehearsing before, like,

258
00:08:47.430 --> 00:08:48.790
at least the week before.

259
00:08:48.790 --> 00:08:49.790
Yeah.

260
00:08:49.990 --> 00:08:51.490
Because that's hard music.

261
00:08:51.690 --> 00:08:54.430
Sondheim is, like, crazy hard music.

262
00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:54.810
Yeah.

263
00:08:55.610 --> 00:08:55.830
Yeah.

264
00:08:56.110 --> 00:09:00.790
Randy told us that on his schedule, he went back and looked in his files.

265
00:09:00.790 --> 00:09:07.270
The only day that you guys had to rehearse all together as a cast was, like, the Monday

266
00:09:07.270 --> 00:09:10.330
morning for half a day at the theater.

267
00:09:10.790 --> 00:09:13.730
And then we all came and started shooting the episode.

268
00:09:14.010 --> 00:09:14.170
Yeah.

269
00:09:14.170 --> 00:09:15.010
Does that sound right?

270
00:09:15.130 --> 00:09:15.950
That does sound right.

271
00:09:15.950 --> 00:09:20.810
But if you, you know, if you're paying attention to the episode, there's not that much of the

272
00:09:20.810 --> 00:09:21.850
play that you see.

273
00:09:22.090 --> 00:09:24.930
You know, like, it's not a ton of production time.

274
00:09:25.350 --> 00:09:29.070
So I think we really only rehearsed a couple of things.

275
00:09:29.250 --> 00:09:32.010
The much more complicated thing was the cold open.

276
00:09:32.130 --> 00:09:32.430
Yeah.

277
00:09:32.550 --> 00:09:32.770
Oh, yeah.

278
00:09:32.770 --> 00:09:35.770
And that, because that was a whole song that we all learned.

279
00:09:35.930 --> 00:09:37.130
And then there was choreography.

280
00:09:37.890 --> 00:09:42.230
It looks, in the execution, it looks spontaneous, like we're just running around the space.

281
00:09:42.330 --> 00:09:44.630
But obviously, it was all meticulously blocked.

282
00:09:45.130 --> 00:09:51.870
And all of the fun, dramatic flourishes that everybody is doing in that scene, that I remember

283
00:09:51.870 --> 00:09:57.250
being quite complicated and learning that song was very hard.

284
00:09:57.470 --> 00:10:01.930
Because, again, Sondheim, he doesn't like to write melodies that you expect.

285
00:10:02.330 --> 00:10:06.890
Everything kind of goes in weird directions and rhythms are weird.

286
00:10:07.210 --> 00:10:15.170
It's like that ends with, like, the cadence is like the demon barber of Fleet Street or

287
00:10:15.170 --> 00:10:18.850
something like, it's like a straight, you know, like a weird hiccup-y thing and everyone

288
00:10:18.850 --> 00:10:19.510
had to get it right.

289
00:10:19.510 --> 00:10:25.550
But, yeah, there was, you were just saying earlier how you are nervous to sing in front

290
00:10:25.550 --> 00:10:26.030
of people.

291
00:10:27.030 --> 00:10:30.350
And I love to sing, obviously.

292
00:10:30.910 --> 00:10:33.130
Anyone who knows me, I love to sing.

293
00:10:33.230 --> 00:10:35.230
But I actually struggle with lyrics.

294
00:10:35.350 --> 00:10:38.450
And so I really have a hard time memorizing lyrics.

295
00:10:38.590 --> 00:10:44.210
And that can then make me really anxious in a performance setting or, like, you know,

296
00:10:44.270 --> 00:10:48.550
if I have to, you know, like there were a few times I had to sing, like, long songs on

297
00:10:48.550 --> 00:10:48.910
the office.

298
00:10:49.230 --> 00:10:52.190
And I, it just, yeah, it puts me in a panic state.

299
00:10:52.410 --> 00:10:54.470
So that one, that cold open was really hard.

300
00:10:55.190 --> 00:10:56.210
But I don't know.

301
00:10:56.330 --> 00:10:57.350
It was enough rehearsal time.

302
00:10:57.830 --> 00:11:01.390
Were you singing live when you guys did that?

303
00:11:01.390 --> 00:11:03.790
Or were you singing to, like, a playback recording?

304
00:11:03.950 --> 00:11:04.870
It seems live.

305
00:11:05.070 --> 00:11:05.570
It is.

306
00:11:05.670 --> 00:11:06.410
It's all live.

307
00:11:06.570 --> 00:11:06.710
Yeah, that's my memory.

308
00:11:06.710 --> 00:11:08.430
And even the stuff in the theater is live.

309
00:11:08.590 --> 00:11:09.390
I thought so.

310
00:11:09.410 --> 00:11:09.690
Yeah.

311
00:11:09.690 --> 00:11:11.170
That was my memory, too.

312
00:11:11.410 --> 00:11:11.630
Yeah.

313
00:11:12.170 --> 00:11:12.410
Oh.

314
00:11:12.730 --> 00:11:16.050
The, the, the, the, the boombox is fake.

315
00:11:16.450 --> 00:11:18.650
Like, there was, you know, obviously that was.

316
00:11:18.790 --> 00:11:19.310
That you hit.

317
00:11:19.410 --> 00:11:20.650
I hit play on the boombox.

318
00:11:20.790 --> 00:11:25.290
And then, but I think they had a, they had a guide track sort of pumped in under the desks

319
00:11:25.290 --> 00:11:28.150
or something that we sang along to.

320
00:11:28.290 --> 00:11:31.010
But, yeah, that was all real singing.

321
00:11:31.410 --> 00:11:35.770
I remember the first take we did of that cold open when you guys came in.

322
00:11:35.770 --> 00:11:37.290
We did not know anything.

323
00:11:37.450 --> 00:11:39.110
We had not seen you rehearse.

324
00:11:39.350 --> 00:11:40.870
It was a complete surprise.

325
00:11:41.330 --> 00:11:44.010
I found it so delightful and amazing.

326
00:11:44.230 --> 00:11:48.430
It was also my first time seeing all of the costumes, which were so well done.

327
00:11:48.550 --> 00:11:48.790
Yeah.

328
00:11:49.370 --> 00:11:50.790
And it was good.

329
00:11:50.950 --> 00:11:52.130
You guys were good.

330
00:11:52.130 --> 00:11:54.610
And I didn't know if it would be good or bad.

331
00:11:54.970 --> 00:12:00.190
And I heard that you actually fought for the production of Sweeney Todd to be good and not bad.

332
00:12:00.350 --> 00:12:01.170
I did.

333
00:12:01.170 --> 00:12:08.470
Yeah, I was very passionate about that because I don't think that, I mean, there are exceptions,

334
00:12:08.750 --> 00:12:15.330
but I generally don't think that, like, intentionally bad performance for comedy works.

335
00:12:15.690 --> 00:12:18.390
It feels, like, cheap and easy and, like.

336
00:12:18.430 --> 00:12:25.930
And so I thought, like, my take on it was, and I was very happy that everyone in production

337
00:12:25.930 --> 00:12:29.610
agreed that, but there was a lot of discussion about it.

338
00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:31.710
But this is obviously an amateur production.

339
00:12:31.870 --> 00:12:34.350
It's a little regional theater production in Scranton, Pennsylvania.

340
00:12:34.730 --> 00:12:41.790
But there's no reason that people in Scranton, Pennsylvania aren't, like, passionate about

341
00:12:41.790 --> 00:12:46.170
musical theater and extremely talented and coming from all walks of life and just really

342
00:12:46.170 --> 00:12:49.850
eager to do this and that maybe there's a really great director in the town who did a

343
00:12:49.850 --> 00:12:51.150
great job casting this thing.

344
00:12:51.150 --> 00:12:57.370
And then when we made the decision to sort of commit to a really good production, that

345
00:12:57.370 --> 00:13:03.110
then also cascaded down into, like, OK, so the costumes are really well done and the sets

346
00:13:03.110 --> 00:13:04.070
are going to look good.

347
00:13:04.510 --> 00:13:10.370
And, you know, Andy has a big fumble with his cell phone in the production, which is kind

348
00:13:10.370 --> 00:13:12.130
of like, that's the mess up.

349
00:13:12.370 --> 00:13:13.450
But it's Andy messing up.

350
00:13:13.450 --> 00:13:19.350
It's not, like, it's not, it's not these, these, like, you know, supporting actors who

351
00:13:19.350 --> 00:13:23.250
are, who came in to, like, you know, really sing and perform for us.

352
00:13:23.330 --> 00:13:24.710
They're not the butt of the joke.

353
00:13:24.970 --> 00:13:25.330
Right, right.

354
00:13:25.570 --> 00:13:29.530
They're part of the fabric of this, the premise of this episode.

355
00:13:29.610 --> 00:13:35.110
And I was excited to honor them and honor that and honor, honor regional theater in that

356
00:13:35.110 --> 00:13:35.390
way.

357
00:13:35.550 --> 00:13:36.650
I love that.

358
00:13:36.790 --> 00:13:39.050
Because, I mean, I've been to great regional theater.

359
00:13:39.050 --> 00:13:43.630
And we've all, like, supported, like, smaller theaters and came up and doing theater.

360
00:13:43.850 --> 00:13:45.710
And I thought that was a great choice.

361
00:13:45.850 --> 00:13:46.870
I'm glad you fought for it.

362
00:13:47.490 --> 00:13:49.790
Yeah, it made it so much more fun, too.

363
00:13:49.990 --> 00:13:50.910
Because, yeah.

364
00:13:51.190 --> 00:13:53.750
I think it also played into Michael's jealousy.

365
00:13:54.010 --> 00:13:57.950
Like, he had to sit through a great production that he wasn't a part of.

366
00:13:58.030 --> 00:13:58.080
Right.

367
00:13:58.410 --> 00:13:58.830
That, you know.

368
00:13:58.910 --> 00:14:00.990
That Daryl's plumber was crushing it.

369
00:14:01.050 --> 00:14:01.410
Yeah.

370
00:14:01.770 --> 00:14:05.790
You know, because you can be a plumber and still kick ass in a musical theater.

371
00:14:06.010 --> 00:14:06.590
That's right.

372
00:14:06.750 --> 00:14:07.010
Yeah.

373
00:14:07.010 --> 00:14:12.710
My favorite thing when Andy's phone went off was the fact that you ruined the whole plot.

374
00:14:12.790 --> 00:14:13.430
Oh, my God.

375
00:14:13.590 --> 00:14:16.890
Like, it wasn't so much that the phone was ringing.

376
00:14:17.150 --> 00:14:19.390
It's how Andy tried to justify it.

377
00:14:19.450 --> 00:14:22.410
And he was ruined the rest of the play.

378
00:14:22.410 --> 00:14:26.430
Well, it's so great because the Sweeney Todd guy gets a huge laugh with, like, his improv.

379
00:14:26.710 --> 00:14:27.610
About the bird chirping.

380
00:14:27.610 --> 00:14:28.770
Yeah, the little fix.

381
00:14:28.930 --> 00:14:31.990
And then Andy just overcompensates, as he always does.

382
00:14:31.990 --> 00:14:33.670
And, like, goes way too far.

383
00:14:33.930 --> 00:14:34.070
Yeah.

384
00:14:34.070 --> 00:14:35.890
And has to pull it back.

385
00:14:35.890 --> 00:14:36.910
I loved it.

386
00:14:37.370 --> 00:14:38.810
It was really actually fun.

387
00:14:38.950 --> 00:14:46.350
I have to say, like, you know, as a fan of musical theater and then just sort of singing production stuff.

388
00:14:46.350 --> 00:14:47.750
Like, it was real.

389
00:14:47.890 --> 00:14:54.950
Those parts of The Office where Andy got to sing and do stuff like that was so fun.

390
00:14:54.950 --> 00:15:02.390
You know, when he serenaded Angela and John Michael Higgins came on board to, like, put that whole a cappella thing together.

391
00:15:03.130 --> 00:15:06.310
Man, it just, like, it's really fun to put that.

392
00:15:06.630 --> 00:15:09.230
You're sort of putting all that work in on the side.

393
00:15:09.550 --> 00:15:11.450
Or you're rehearsing in your trailer.

394
00:15:11.630 --> 00:15:15.570
You're getting everything kind of like, you know, you want it to be good and you want it to be tight.

395
00:15:15.570 --> 00:15:19.430
And then you get to perform it for the cast and do it.

396
00:15:19.430 --> 00:15:21.370
There was the Closing Time bar song.

397
00:15:21.490 --> 00:15:21.910
Yeah.

398
00:15:22.450 --> 00:15:22.870
Yeah.

399
00:15:22.930 --> 00:15:26.810
There are just a lot of great musical moments throughout the show.

400
00:15:27.310 --> 00:15:33.410
You have one of my favorite lines in this episode, Jenna, which was when you guys check in with Ellie on the phone.

401
00:15:33.410 --> 00:15:40.370
And you're like, yeah, no, it's great to see Andy singing in the right context or something like that.

402
00:15:40.830 --> 00:15:42.490
I forgot what the line is, but like.

403
00:15:42.530 --> 00:15:42.730
Yeah.

404
00:15:42.750 --> 00:15:43.550
No, that is it.

405
00:15:43.650 --> 00:15:55.450
It's so perfect because it is, that is, it actually, I agreed, re-watching the episode, I was, you know, I thought it was, it was nice to see Andy singing in this context.

406
00:15:55.450 --> 00:16:00.510
It's usually so intrusive and so like, you know, and that's part of why it's funny sometimes.

407
00:16:00.510 --> 00:16:04.430
But in this context, it actually was kind of, it was nice.

408
00:16:04.450 --> 00:16:09.070
And it was part of something that was supposed to be that way.

409
00:16:09.310 --> 00:16:20.230
And it really, it gave Andy, I think, a sort of authenticity in this episode that he really struggles with in most of the Dunder Mifflin universe.

410
00:16:20.630 --> 00:16:23.210
Well, I loved the ending of this episode.

411
00:16:23.430 --> 00:16:26.510
I think I re-watched it, I don't know, three or four times.

412
00:16:26.510 --> 00:16:27.930
It made me happy.

413
00:16:27.930 --> 00:16:29.430
It made my heart happy.

414
00:16:29.430 --> 00:16:34.990
And Daryl is playing Macy Gray's I Try on the piano, and Andy is just belting it out.

415
00:16:35.130 --> 00:16:41.450
And you have this montage of all this group of people and where they're at in life and dealing with whatever it is they're dealing.

416
00:16:41.690 --> 00:16:48.710
But you're singing, Ed, in that moment as Andy is like, it's like you're halftime at the Super Bowl, man.

417
00:16:48.890 --> 00:16:51.870
You're just like everything, your whole body is into it.

418
00:16:51.970 --> 00:16:53.410
And it made me so happy.

419
00:16:53.610 --> 00:16:54.810
I just love that moment.

420
00:16:55.550 --> 00:17:02.610
Randy told us that the original song for the end was My Heart Will Go On, the Titanic song.

421
00:17:02.750 --> 00:17:04.050
That rings a bell, yeah.

422
00:17:04.330 --> 00:17:07.850
He said you even had to go into a studio and record it.

423
00:17:08.130 --> 00:17:10.850
And he has, like, the only recording.

424
00:17:11.050 --> 00:17:11.550
What?

425
00:17:11.910 --> 00:17:12.590
Of it?

426
00:17:12.730 --> 00:17:13.230
Of you.

427
00:17:13.230 --> 00:17:20.110
We haven't heard it, but he said there is this impassioned version of you singing, My Heart Will Go On.

428
00:17:20.210 --> 00:17:23.030
And then they couldn't get the rights to it, and they had to change it.

429
00:17:23.310 --> 00:17:28.730
I vaguely remember that, but I don't remember recording it or, but man, I want to hear that.

430
00:17:28.950 --> 00:17:29.830
I know, so do I.

431
00:17:29.830 --> 00:17:30.530
We'll get it for you from Randy.

432
00:17:30.690 --> 00:17:30.910
Yeah.

433
00:17:30.910 --> 00:17:33.490
He has a vault of pretty amazing things.

434
00:17:33.750 --> 00:17:39.730
Is there any moment from this episode for you that, like, stood out for Andy as, like, one of your favorite moments?

435
00:17:40.010 --> 00:17:42.970
I think there's a lot of really cool stuff in this episode.

436
00:17:43.450 --> 00:17:46.010
The cold open is just so fun.

437
00:17:46.330 --> 00:17:49.550
That's a really special, special thing.

438
00:17:49.550 --> 00:18:01.310
And it's Andy kind of, like, it's a rare moment of Andy just living in his skin with so much passion and self-confidence, you know?

439
00:18:01.770 --> 00:18:03.630
And that's really, really cool.

440
00:18:03.870 --> 00:18:11.270
I think that I was proud of how the, it's very short, but the musical, the solo.

441
00:18:11.790 --> 00:18:12.310
Joanna.

442
00:18:12.490 --> 00:18:12.770
Joanna.

443
00:18:13.010 --> 00:18:13.630
Yeah, yeah.

444
00:18:13.630 --> 00:18:19.650
I put a lot of work into that and was really proud of how that turned out.

445
00:18:20.650 --> 00:18:24.690
Narratively, the Aaron stuff is really poignant.

446
00:18:25.130 --> 00:18:27.210
I thought that was really sweet.

447
00:18:27.650 --> 00:18:30.650
You know, Aaron is just so detached.

448
00:18:31.330 --> 00:18:41.370
So, like, but it's, but she's clearly tapping into some longing for Andy by showing up and then him kind of getting it at the end and just being like,

449
00:18:41.370 --> 00:18:43.890
this, yeah, this is not, not good.

450
00:18:44.190 --> 00:18:44.630
Yeah.

451
00:18:44.970 --> 00:18:51.910
I loved Andy's earnestness when Aaron had to take the babysitting job when he's like, I get it.

452
00:18:51.990 --> 00:18:53.130
It's a huge opportunity.

453
00:18:53.530 --> 00:18:54.070
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

454
00:18:56.530 --> 00:18:57.690
Oh, my gosh.

455
00:18:57.790 --> 00:18:59.890
He's checking his phone backstage.

456
00:19:00.590 --> 00:19:02.150
One of the most brilliant.

457
00:19:02.950 --> 00:19:09.610
Okay, so this is actually funny because I was saying before how I generally think that performing badly is a cheap joke.

458
00:19:09.610 --> 00:19:16.590
Like, if it's, if you're performing, you know, in a performance context and you're doing it badly on purpose.

459
00:19:16.770 --> 00:19:20.570
I don't like that usually, but the tag of this episode.

460
00:19:20.670 --> 00:19:21.850
Oh, my gosh.

461
00:19:22.210 --> 00:19:23.170
Of Michael Scott doing.

462
00:19:23.630 --> 00:19:24.670
Law and order.

463
00:19:25.150 --> 00:19:26.210
An entire episode.

464
00:19:26.350 --> 00:19:27.290
So badly.

465
00:19:27.290 --> 00:19:27.830
As a monologue.

466
00:19:27.990 --> 00:19:28.530
So badly.

467
00:19:28.890 --> 00:19:31.130
It's so funny.

468
00:19:31.410 --> 00:19:34.070
I watched that four or five times.

469
00:19:34.310 --> 00:19:36.250
It's just, it's priceless.

470
00:19:36.250 --> 00:19:40.610
But how many people could do that and sell it but Steve, you know?

471
00:19:40.890 --> 00:19:43.030
Because that, I believed it.

472
00:19:43.110 --> 00:19:46.170
I believed every second that that's how Michael Scott would have done it.

473
00:19:46.230 --> 00:19:46.970
A hundred percent.

474
00:19:47.210 --> 00:19:51.750
And, you know, Steve almost never broke on, I mean, he very rarely broke.

475
00:19:51.810 --> 00:19:51.970
Right.

476
00:19:52.450 --> 00:19:55.690
And I was watching that being like, he had to be cracking himself up.

477
00:19:55.870 --> 00:20:00.250
Like, this was, there were, I guarantee you, there were a few times he broke, you know.

478
00:20:00.250 --> 00:20:05.430
When he was slinging, I don't know, what, is it a belt or a sash or something as the lady?

479
00:20:05.570 --> 00:20:05.830
Yeah.

480
00:20:05.970 --> 00:20:07.430
I know he broke doing that.

481
00:20:07.530 --> 00:20:09.530
I know he had to have cracked himself up.

482
00:20:09.570 --> 00:20:13.490
Or singing the theme song, like, da, da, da, da, da.

483
00:20:14.330 --> 00:20:16.030
Just, oh, my God.

484
00:20:16.170 --> 00:20:16.670
I know.

485
00:20:16.910 --> 00:20:17.350
Priceless.

486
00:20:18.150 --> 00:20:23.850
Do you guys, I'm curious, like, we made so many Office episodes.

487
00:20:23.850 --> 00:20:33.290
And it's really funny to me the way fans understand the episodes as these, like, very tight, coherent stories.

488
00:20:34.590 --> 00:20:46.730
Whereas I find that from our perspective as the actors, in looking at these stories from a production standpoint, it takes us a week to shoot a half hour episode.

489
00:20:46.990 --> 00:20:50.890
And then we go right into the next week of another episode.

490
00:20:51.810 --> 00:20:56.750
And when you look back on it, it's just kind of this wash of work, right?

491
00:20:56.910 --> 00:21:04.550
And I don't, for me, it's very hard to kind of remember the specifics of episodes and the specifics of, like, what was in what episode.

492
00:21:05.150 --> 00:21:11.450
And, I mean, you guys have been really kind of studying them, so it's probably more front of mind for you.

493
00:21:11.450 --> 00:21:21.690
But I found this, going back and watching this episode, like, it did trigger a lot of memories.

494
00:21:21.690 --> 00:21:28.210
But it also was a little frustrating kind of realizing that what I don't remember.

495
00:21:28.470 --> 00:21:29.250
And there are certain scenes.

496
00:21:29.250 --> 00:21:31.450
I don't know if you have this experience watching episodes of The Office.

497
00:21:31.490 --> 00:21:32.210
All the time.

498
00:21:32.250 --> 00:21:34.150
Where you're like, I don't remember shooting that.

499
00:21:34.310 --> 00:21:34.890
I don't remember.

500
00:21:34.890 --> 00:21:37.170
I don't even remember that day or what was going on.

501
00:21:37.170 --> 00:21:41.390
And sometimes it's because you have some crazy thing going on in your life.

502
00:21:41.490 --> 00:21:45.390
But more often, I think it's just because you just don't remember every day at work.

503
00:21:46.350 --> 00:21:48.910
And I got a little heart sick kind of watching.

504
00:21:49.090 --> 00:21:58.330
There were some parts of this episode where I was like, oh, boy, I wish I remembered, like, just, you know, that scene where we're all around the piano together.

505
00:21:58.330 --> 00:22:03.390
And it's one of those rare scenes where almost everybody is, like, in a clump.

506
00:22:03.530 --> 00:22:05.750
And it's with positive energy, right?

507
00:22:05.870 --> 00:22:10.190
Usually it's like, you know, a meeting in the conference room.

508
00:22:10.490 --> 00:22:10.540
Yeah.

509
00:22:10.730 --> 00:22:11.130
It's all crazy.

510
00:22:11.310 --> 00:22:15.890
But, like, to have a moment where everyone's kind of in a good place and happy.

511
00:22:15.990 --> 00:22:20.670
It just, I remember watching that and thinking, like, this is so special.

512
00:22:20.670 --> 00:22:22.130
Like, I love these people.

513
00:22:22.130 --> 00:22:23.310
I love these human beings.

514
00:22:24.150 --> 00:22:27.610
And it made me a little bit heart sick for the things I don't remember.

515
00:22:28.210 --> 00:22:32.650
You're literally, your character has the line at the end where you say.

516
00:22:32.870 --> 00:22:33.290
In the finale.

517
00:22:33.610 --> 00:22:37.150
You wish that you knew you were in the good times when you were in them.

518
00:22:37.470 --> 00:22:38.990
Like, that's literally what you're saying.

519
00:22:39.130 --> 00:22:39.890
It's so true, yeah.

520
00:22:40.070 --> 00:22:40.770
But I get it.

521
00:22:40.790 --> 00:22:43.510
It is true, though, because you work every day.

522
00:22:43.730 --> 00:22:45.690
And some days stand out and some days don't.

523
00:22:45.850 --> 00:22:47.290
It doesn't matter what your job is.

524
00:22:47.550 --> 00:22:49.050
And acting is no different.

525
00:22:49.050 --> 00:22:55.130
But sometimes I don't know the show the way people know it when they can just sit down

526
00:22:55.130 --> 00:22:56.610
and watch it all the way through.

527
00:22:56.950 --> 00:22:57.390
Right, right.

528
00:22:57.530 --> 00:22:59.770
And we cross-borded episodes.

529
00:23:00.130 --> 00:23:00.370
Yeah, of course.

530
00:23:00.370 --> 00:23:04.770
Sometimes we shot three days in one episode and two days in another episode.

531
00:23:04.870 --> 00:23:05.010
Right.

532
00:23:05.010 --> 00:23:09.450
And it kind of becomes like when your kids mix all the Play-Doh together, you know,

533
00:23:09.570 --> 00:23:12.450
and you get that one big Play-Doh mound.

534
00:23:12.610 --> 00:23:12.950
I don't know.

535
00:23:13.050 --> 00:23:13.970
That's not very articulate.

536
00:23:14.370 --> 00:23:15.710
I liked that metaphor.

537
00:23:15.950 --> 00:23:16.370
I do, too.

538
00:23:16.370 --> 00:23:22.490
I feel like every conference room scene is one memory in my head.

539
00:23:22.610 --> 00:23:25.870
So many of these episodes I hadn't seen since we filmed them.

540
00:23:26.090 --> 00:23:26.410
Crazy.

541
00:23:26.610 --> 00:23:29.910
But I'm so thankful for, like, a wonderful thing that we have.

542
00:23:30.310 --> 00:23:30.830
Amen.

543
00:23:31.210 --> 00:23:31.470
Yeah.

544
00:23:31.670 --> 00:23:38.250
And when I do catch an episode here and there, it is like just hanging out with old friends.

545
00:23:38.510 --> 00:23:38.810
Yeah.

546
00:23:38.850 --> 00:23:39.710
It's just awesome.

547
00:23:39.710 --> 00:23:46.770
I don't think I ever lost the feeling of excitement walking onto set about what was

548
00:23:46.770 --> 00:23:47.450
going to happen.

549
00:23:47.630 --> 00:23:53.130
Like, what are these hilarious, wonderful people going to do to surprise me today?

550
00:23:54.270 --> 00:23:58.570
And like you were saying, conference room scenes, like, all of us getting in there, just being

551
00:23:58.570 --> 00:24:00.470
like this, something funny is going to happen.

552
00:24:00.730 --> 00:24:01.990
Like, somebody is going to do something.

553
00:24:02.070 --> 00:24:02.850
We all have the script.

554
00:24:02.990 --> 00:24:03.730
We know what we're going to say.

555
00:24:03.850 --> 00:24:06.970
But it's still like, I can't wait to see how BJ does this line.

556
00:24:07.030 --> 00:24:09.730
Or I can't wait to see how Oscar does this line.

557
00:24:09.850 --> 00:24:13.010
And then, you know, take after take, it's just so warm and funny.

558
00:24:13.010 --> 00:24:18.490
And there are, of course, times that you get run down and you get tired and cranky.

559
00:24:18.610 --> 00:24:27.310
But I always, always would walk into that soundstage, like, feeling excited and remembering back.

560
00:24:27.390 --> 00:24:28.050
I mean, years.

561
00:24:28.210 --> 00:24:28.830
How many years?

562
00:24:29.030 --> 00:24:33.010
I worked on the show less than you guys, but seven years.

563
00:24:33.230 --> 00:24:33.570
Almost as long.

564
00:24:33.670 --> 00:24:33.830
Yeah.

565
00:24:34.010 --> 00:24:34.450
Seven years.

566
00:24:34.450 --> 00:24:40.750
And it just always walking on that stage was, was so like, just, just so fun.

567
00:24:40.950 --> 00:24:43.290
And like, what's going to happen?

568
00:24:43.830 --> 00:24:48.230
Well, Ed, before we let you go, can you tell us a little bit about your music?

569
00:24:48.750 --> 00:24:52.450
Are you performing anywhere with your banjo?

570
00:24:52.750 --> 00:24:54.550
Are you still doing the bluegrass situation?

571
00:24:55.430 --> 00:24:55.990
Yeah.

572
00:24:56.190 --> 00:25:01.270
So the bluegrasssituation.com is still very much alive and well.

573
00:25:01.270 --> 00:25:11.510
And that's a, that's just kind of a roots and folk, bluegrass music, kind of community, news, blog, hub.

574
00:25:11.890 --> 00:25:13.010
And that's great.

575
00:25:13.290 --> 00:25:17.670
I have pulled back from performing a lot in the last couple of years.

576
00:25:17.670 --> 00:25:28.070
I had kids and I'm just kind of focused elsewhere, but I still play all the time, especially for my kids, because they're a captive audience and they can't walk away.

577
00:25:28.070 --> 00:25:33.150
Um, and, and I'm eager to start getting back out there.

578
00:25:33.430 --> 00:25:33.830
Yeah.

579
00:25:33.970 --> 00:25:40.270
I've been, I've been playing a lot and I'm starting to kind of get more serious about practicing and, and, and rehearsing,

580
00:25:40.350 --> 00:25:44.910
but there's not much going on in the professional space at this point.

581
00:25:45.090 --> 00:25:46.350
Except Rutherford Falls.

582
00:25:46.610 --> 00:25:46.890
Oh yeah.

583
00:25:46.910 --> 00:25:48.710
Season two is out now on Peacock.

584
00:25:48.710 --> 00:25:49.430
Yes, indeed.

585
00:25:49.650 --> 00:25:50.870
Rutherford Falls season two.

586
00:25:51.310 --> 00:26:01.170
Very, very proud of this show, uh, which I co-created with, um, uh, an, an amazing showrunner, Sierra Teller Ornelas and Michael Schur.

587
00:26:01.450 --> 00:26:01.770
Yes.

588
00:26:02.190 --> 00:26:03.990
Who, uh, Office fans know well.

589
00:26:04.770 --> 00:26:05.730
That's going on.

590
00:26:05.990 --> 00:26:06.750
We love it.

591
00:26:06.990 --> 00:26:07.590
We love it.

592
00:26:07.730 --> 00:26:09.210
Ed, I want to say two things to you.

593
00:26:09.990 --> 00:26:11.350
Banjo kids album.

594
00:26:12.270 --> 00:26:12.790
Right?

595
00:26:13.070 --> 00:26:15.450
That's a, that is a, that has crossed my mind.

596
00:26:15.450 --> 00:26:19.790
I've been writing a lot of music lately and I think I'm going to, I, I, I just, I think.

597
00:26:20.030 --> 00:26:24.530
I would have loved that when Isabel was little because, you know, I, I don't know.

598
00:26:24.690 --> 00:26:27.090
I had all the same songs that everyone had had forever.

599
00:26:27.410 --> 00:26:32.590
And I, anytime there was a new kid's song that had any kind of a fun tune.

600
00:26:32.790 --> 00:26:33.390
Oh my gosh.

601
00:26:33.490 --> 00:26:34.390
I got so excited.

602
00:26:34.990 --> 00:26:35.430
All right.

603
00:26:35.970 --> 00:26:36.270
All right.

604
00:26:36.290 --> 00:26:38.490
That might be the push I needed to get it done.

605
00:26:38.830 --> 00:26:39.070
Okay.

606
00:26:40.370 --> 00:26:40.930
All right.

607
00:26:41.050 --> 00:26:42.110
Ed, we love you so much.

608
00:26:42.230 --> 00:26:44.630
Thank you so much for coming in the studio.

609
00:26:44.630 --> 00:26:46.470
Yes, Ed is in here, you guys.

610
00:26:46.470 --> 00:26:46.930
I'm in the studio.

611
00:26:46.950 --> 00:26:48.030
He's sitting next to us.

612
00:26:48.150 --> 00:26:49.210
This is the table.

613
00:26:49.370 --> 00:26:50.590
Yes, we're here.

614
00:26:51.090 --> 00:26:52.150
Thanks so much for having me.

615
00:26:52.330 --> 00:26:54.590
It's just so good to see you guys.

616
00:26:54.970 --> 00:26:55.590
It's wonderful.

617
00:26:55.910 --> 00:26:56.530
Warm my heart.

618
00:26:56.630 --> 00:26:57.030
I love you.

619
00:26:57.490 --> 00:26:58.710
Well, we're going to take a break.

620
00:26:58.710 --> 00:27:01.870
We're going to walk Ed out and then we'll be back to break down this episode.

621
00:27:12.030 --> 00:27:14.790
Angela, I'm just going to be happy all day now because we got to hang out with Ed.

622
00:27:14.970 --> 00:27:15.430
I know.

623
00:27:15.890 --> 00:27:19.510
We should tell you guys, we've actually been chatting with him for like an hour.

624
00:27:19.830 --> 00:27:22.030
We talked to Ed for two hours.

625
00:27:22.270 --> 00:27:22.530
Yeah.

626
00:27:22.850 --> 00:27:23.790
I didn't want him to leave.

627
00:27:23.790 --> 00:27:24.370
Me either.

628
00:27:24.650 --> 00:27:26.770
And we did finally have to let him go.

629
00:27:27.070 --> 00:27:27.170
Yeah.

630
00:27:27.430 --> 00:27:28.850
We have to finish this episode.

631
00:27:29.050 --> 00:27:29.230
Yeah.

632
00:27:29.990 --> 00:27:35.950
Also during our break, we reached out to Randy and you guys, he's sending Cassie the audio

633
00:27:35.950 --> 00:27:39.790
file of Ed singing, My Heart Will Go On, right now.

634
00:27:40.030 --> 00:27:41.890
We are going to play it at the end of the episode.

635
00:27:41.890 --> 00:27:42.650
I can't wait.

636
00:27:42.910 --> 00:27:43.110
Yes.

637
00:27:43.330 --> 00:27:44.990
Definitely stick around to hear this.

638
00:27:45.190 --> 00:27:49.450
We heard it and it is truly, truly amazing.

639
00:27:49.630 --> 00:27:50.650
Like I got chills.

640
00:27:50.910 --> 00:27:51.310
I know.

641
00:27:51.310 --> 00:27:52.430
It was kind of a moment.

642
00:27:52.590 --> 00:27:54.490
We're going to play the whole entire song.

643
00:27:54.670 --> 00:27:55.950
Ed gave us his permission.

644
00:27:56.910 --> 00:27:57.310
Enjoy.

645
00:27:57.650 --> 00:27:58.450
But stick to the end.

646
00:27:58.510 --> 00:27:58.890
It's there.

647
00:27:59.010 --> 00:27:59.770
It'll be at the end.

648
00:28:00.130 --> 00:28:00.530
Okay.

649
00:28:01.330 --> 00:28:03.050
Let's start breaking down this episode.

650
00:28:03.550 --> 00:28:04.950
We talked about this a little bit.

651
00:28:05.070 --> 00:28:07.850
This episode opens with this fantastic cold open.

652
00:28:08.010 --> 00:28:12.190
This performance of a musical number from Sweeney Todd surprises the whole bullpen.

653
00:28:12.190 --> 00:28:12.850
Yeah.

654
00:28:13.330 --> 00:28:16.630
And Michael is like, oh my gosh, I auditioned for this.

655
00:28:16.770 --> 00:28:18.470
When did the cast list go up?

656
00:28:18.550 --> 00:28:19.710
What part did I get?

657
00:28:19.970 --> 00:28:21.510
How clueless is Michael?

658
00:28:21.910 --> 00:28:24.190
We got a fan catch from Carrie F.

659
00:28:24.370 --> 00:28:29.190
in Japan who said, when Michael asks about the cast list,

660
00:28:29.370 --> 00:28:31.550
look at Creed's face in the background.

661
00:28:31.550 --> 00:28:32.610
It is amazing.

662
00:28:32.610 --> 00:28:35.590
And I feel like everyone should pause to see it.

663
00:28:35.830 --> 00:28:36.630
Oh my goodness.

664
00:28:36.790 --> 00:28:37.510
Did you look at it?

665
00:28:37.510 --> 00:28:38.150
I did.

666
00:28:38.390 --> 00:28:39.210
What's he doing?

667
00:28:40.630 --> 00:28:43.550
It's hard to explain, but it is a great look.

668
00:28:43.990 --> 00:28:49.330
He looks almost like he's responsible for not casting Michael.

669
00:28:49.610 --> 00:28:51.190
Like, I don't want to...

670
00:28:51.190 --> 00:28:53.190
It was his decision.

671
00:28:53.850 --> 00:28:54.890
It's amazing.

672
00:28:55.190 --> 00:28:55.490
All right.

673
00:28:55.530 --> 00:28:56.510
I have to go check that out.

674
00:28:56.630 --> 00:28:57.150
Thanks, Carrie.

675
00:28:57.530 --> 00:28:59.850
I was so impressed with this opening musical number.

676
00:28:59.970 --> 00:29:01.570
I really thought we should hear these guys.

677
00:29:01.830 --> 00:29:02.630
Oh, let's do it.

678
00:29:03.890 --> 00:29:04.410
Annie?

679
00:29:08.240 --> 00:29:10.280
Attend the tale of Sweeney Todd.

680
00:29:11.780 --> 00:29:14.580
His skin was pale and his eye was odd.

681
00:29:15.380 --> 00:29:16.180
Yes, let's do it.

682
00:29:16.180 --> 00:29:19.820
He gave the faces of gentle men who never thereafter were heard of again.

683
00:29:20.420 --> 00:29:23.060
He trod a path that few have trod.

684
00:29:23.420 --> 00:29:24.660
It's Sweeney Todd.

685
00:29:26.180 --> 00:29:28.360
The demon barber of Fleet Street.

686
00:29:29.340 --> 00:29:30.440
Did you write this?

687
00:29:30.920 --> 00:29:31.240
No.

688
00:29:31.600 --> 00:29:32.020
Who did?

689
00:29:32.120 --> 00:29:32.680
Stephen Sondheim.

690
00:29:32.900 --> 00:29:33.480
Who is he?

691
00:29:33.480 --> 00:29:36.920
Swing your razor wide.

692
00:29:37.140 --> 00:29:40.320
Sweeney, hold it to the sky.

693
00:29:41.540 --> 00:29:56.610
A fan question from Miriam Ellen Utah who wanted to know,

694
00:29:56.810 --> 00:29:59.870
how much did NBC pay in royalties for Sweeney Todd?

695
00:30:00.490 --> 00:30:06.990
Miriam said, typical grand rights can cost a small high school production thousands of dollars.

696
00:30:07.290 --> 00:30:08.570
What did you guys pay?

697
00:30:08.570 --> 00:30:13.370
Well, I checked with Randy Cordray, of course, and he said this really was a huge deal.

698
00:30:13.850 --> 00:30:19.190
Quote, we basically had to produce a full-blown community theater production of Sweeney Todd

699
00:30:19.190 --> 00:30:26.750
embedded into a full-blown episode of The Office, and negotiating the music rights was complicated.

700
00:30:27.370 --> 00:30:34.430
He said, Eric Colgen, our post-producer, contacted the Universal Television Music Supervisor,

701
00:30:34.690 --> 00:30:37.470
Carrie Druton, about getting the sync rights.

702
00:30:38.110 --> 00:30:40.950
That meant that we were allowed to sing the song ourself.

703
00:30:40.950 --> 00:30:43.910
We weren't going to, like, play a recorded soundtrack.

704
00:30:44.290 --> 00:30:46.010
Yeah, there's all these layers to it.

705
00:30:46.170 --> 00:30:52.050
But we also had to get something called grand rights because we were going to air it on television.

706
00:30:52.050 --> 00:30:52.890
Oh.

707
00:30:53.330 --> 00:30:58.810
There's a difference between just doing it on a stage and then committing it to television forever.

708
00:31:00.230 --> 00:31:02.770
Stephen Sondheim had to sign off.

709
00:31:03.270 --> 00:31:04.910
They had to send him our script.

710
00:31:05.750 --> 00:31:06.210
Really?

711
00:31:06.490 --> 00:31:06.810
Mm-hmm.

712
00:31:07.150 --> 00:31:08.310
He read our script?

713
00:31:08.370 --> 00:31:09.530
He read Andy's play.

714
00:31:09.850 --> 00:31:10.830
Oh, my gosh.

715
00:31:10.910 --> 00:31:11.450
Guess what?

716
00:31:11.630 --> 00:31:11.890
What?

717
00:31:12.130 --> 00:31:12.950
He loved it.

718
00:31:13.090 --> 00:31:13.470
Aw!

719
00:31:13.770 --> 00:31:15.150
He gave us his blessing.

720
00:31:16.490 --> 00:31:23.730
Ultimately, we ended up paying $100,000, and we got the rights to sing four songs.

721
00:31:24.230 --> 00:31:25.470
And that's how we did it.

722
00:31:25.690 --> 00:31:25.970
Wow.

723
00:31:26.350 --> 00:31:28.650
I have a fan question from Sarah L.

724
00:31:28.890 --> 00:31:30.210
in San Antonio, Texas.

725
00:31:30.210 --> 00:31:30.990
What's up, Sarah?

726
00:31:31.150 --> 00:31:31.730
Hi, Sarah.

727
00:31:32.250 --> 00:31:34.390
Where did the singers come from for this episode?

728
00:31:34.570 --> 00:31:36.050
Sarah, I love this question.

729
00:31:36.230 --> 00:31:37.210
We'll tell you all about it.

730
00:31:37.370 --> 00:31:37.590
Yeah.

731
00:31:37.810 --> 00:31:40.890
All the singers were cast by our casting director, Allison Jones.

732
00:31:40.890 --> 00:31:44.470
And our director for this episode was John Scott.

733
00:31:44.910 --> 00:31:51.470
Before he directed The Office, he had directed episodes of Glee, and he'd also worked as a camera operator and cinematographer.

734
00:31:51.710 --> 00:31:59.290
So this kind of made him perfect because he knew how to blend musical performance and documentary-style shooting.

735
00:31:59.670 --> 00:32:03.950
This is the only episode of The Office that he directed, and thank goodness we got him for it.

736
00:32:04.070 --> 00:32:04.510
I know.

737
00:32:04.670 --> 00:32:05.690
He's a prolific director.

738
00:32:05.690 --> 00:32:07.410
If you look him up, he's just done everything.

739
00:32:07.410 --> 00:32:13.170
We also hired a musical supervisor for this episode, who Randy called a musical genius.

740
00:32:13.550 --> 00:32:15.230
His name was Nathan Wang.

741
00:32:15.510 --> 00:32:23.590
He prepped all of the musical background tracks, attended all rehearsals, and he sat in on the auditions with Allison Jones and John Scott.

742
00:32:23.790 --> 00:32:26.430
So the three of them found all of our singers.

743
00:32:26.810 --> 00:32:27.110
Yes.

744
00:32:27.310 --> 00:32:32.930
And Nathan, if you listen to this, Randy could not say enough wonderful things about you.

745
00:32:33.070 --> 00:32:36.390
He just went on and on about how important you were for this episode.

746
00:32:36.390 --> 00:32:37.230
Yes.

747
00:32:37.490 --> 00:32:42.890
Randy also told us that this cold-open sequence was choreographed by Mary Ann Kellogg.

748
00:32:43.010 --> 00:32:45.010
You guys remember she did the lip dub.

749
00:32:45.250 --> 00:32:46.850
And here were the performers.

750
00:32:47.230 --> 00:32:49.270
Kelly Ebsery played the Beggar Lady.

751
00:32:49.530 --> 00:32:51.170
Maxwell Glick played Tobias.

752
00:32:51.630 --> 00:32:53.930
Heather Marie Marsden played Mrs. Lovett.

753
00:32:54.270 --> 00:32:56.590
David Kirk Grant played Castmate No.

754
00:32:56.710 --> 00:32:56.990
2.

755
00:32:57.670 --> 00:32:59.710
Erika Hanrahan Ball played Joanna.

756
00:33:00.470 --> 00:33:02.410
Harry Murphy played Judge Turpin.

757
00:33:02.890 --> 00:33:04.690
Randy Gaia played Pirelli.

758
00:33:04.690 --> 00:33:07.390
And Michael Tuba Heatherton played Beatle.

759
00:33:07.610 --> 00:33:08.830
What a fantastic group.

760
00:33:09.110 --> 00:33:11.350
And lady, how fun was it to see Tuba?

761
00:33:11.590 --> 00:33:12.490
I know.

762
00:33:12.710 --> 00:33:13.450
I know.

763
00:33:13.570 --> 00:33:17.430
We should tell everybody who he is because he appeared on The Office several times.

764
00:33:17.590 --> 00:33:20.110
We even got a fan catch from Rob B.

765
00:33:20.190 --> 00:33:22.870
in Los Angeles, California, who said,

766
00:33:23.130 --> 00:33:25.430
Ladies, I think you need a Tuba Tracker.

767
00:33:25.670 --> 00:33:26.950
Oh, a Tuba Tracker!

768
00:33:26.950 --> 00:33:32.890
This episode marks the first appearance of Michael Tuba Heatherton in the series.

769
00:33:33.370 --> 00:33:39.930
And he reprises this role in a deleted scene from Season 8, Episode 13, Jury Duty,

770
00:33:40.150 --> 00:33:47.250
where he pretends to be a police officer, only to be recognized by Daryl as a member of Andy's play.

771
00:33:47.250 --> 00:33:50.150
I look forward to that deleted scene, lady.

772
00:33:50.390 --> 00:33:50.670
Uh-huh.

773
00:33:50.670 --> 00:33:57.330
But we will also see Tuba in The Seminar as an attendee of Andy's seminar.

774
00:33:57.830 --> 00:34:05.910
And we're going to see two of Tuba's clown headshots in Roseanne's Talent Agency in Season 9.

775
00:34:05.910 --> 00:34:15.510
And finally, we're going to see him as the minister in Season 9, The Farm, and in the finale, marrying Dwight and Angela.

776
00:34:16.230 --> 00:34:18.370
He is just like Nelson Franklin.

777
00:34:18.690 --> 00:34:20.870
He is, because he played multiple roles.

778
00:34:20.910 --> 00:34:21.470
Multiple roles.

779
00:34:21.550 --> 00:34:22.590
Or maybe the same.

780
00:34:23.010 --> 00:34:26.130
Maybe he's a man who has many jobs.

781
00:34:26.450 --> 00:34:26.810
Yeah.

782
00:34:27.090 --> 00:34:29.230
Like the character Kirk on Gilmore Girls.

783
00:34:29.470 --> 00:34:30.550
He worked everywhere.

784
00:34:30.810 --> 00:34:32.390
I have never seen Gilmore Girls.

785
00:34:32.530 --> 00:34:33.450
Whoa, really?

786
00:34:33.770 --> 00:34:34.050
Yeah.

787
00:34:34.410 --> 00:34:35.790
Oh, they talk real faSt.

788
00:34:35.910 --> 00:34:36.550
They do?

789
00:34:36.690 --> 00:34:36.950
Mm-hmm.

790
00:34:37.050 --> 00:34:37.990
Yeah, that was the thing.

791
00:34:38.130 --> 00:34:40.030
The dialogue, it comes out very, very faSt.

792
00:34:40.770 --> 00:34:41.170
Oh.

793
00:34:41.890 --> 00:34:42.170
Mm-hmm.

794
00:34:42.370 --> 00:34:43.590
Just a style choice?

795
00:34:43.730 --> 00:34:43.990
Yes.

796
00:34:44.510 --> 00:34:45.090
I don't think I...

797
00:34:45.090 --> 00:34:47.610
And you had to say your lines exactly as written.

798
00:34:48.370 --> 00:34:50.070
I would not do very well.

799
00:34:50.910 --> 00:34:51.990
This is not the show.

800
00:34:52.070 --> 00:34:53.430
It's good you were not cast on it.

801
00:34:53.470 --> 00:34:54.190
No, I was not.

802
00:34:54.930 --> 00:34:55.850
Well, Rob B.

803
00:34:55.950 --> 00:34:57.570
from Los Angeles wants to know,

804
00:34:57.730 --> 00:35:03.310
is it just a happy coincidence that Tuba was cast in so many one-off roles on The Office?

805
00:35:03.770 --> 00:35:05.190
Or is there another reason?

806
00:35:05.570 --> 00:35:07.010
There is another reason.

807
00:35:07.450 --> 00:35:09.190
First of all, Tuba's amazing.

808
00:35:09.430 --> 00:35:10.550
We love him.

809
00:35:10.770 --> 00:35:16.330
In addition to being extremely talented, Tuba was also married to our amazing script supervisor,

810
00:35:16.690 --> 00:35:16.990
Theda.

811
00:35:16.990 --> 00:35:17.570
Yeah.

812
00:35:18.310 --> 00:35:19.330
And Tuba was an actor.

813
00:35:19.490 --> 00:35:20.810
He was a professional clown.

814
00:35:20.990 --> 00:35:22.950
Just an amazing person.

815
00:35:23.190 --> 00:35:29.570
Well, let's get into this episode because Andy has two comps for closing night of his show for Erin and her Gabe.

816
00:35:29.570 --> 00:35:31.910
And he finds out Gabe isn't going.

817
00:35:32.070 --> 00:35:33.470
He didn't think anyone else was going to go.

818
00:35:34.290 --> 00:35:35.570
How happy is Andy?

819
00:35:35.930 --> 00:35:40.450
He is so happy until it looks like nobody else is coming.

820
00:35:41.210 --> 00:35:41.730
Like...

821
00:35:41.730 --> 00:35:42.070
Yeah.

822
00:35:42.070 --> 00:35:44.330
Jim and Pam's babysitter fell through.

823
00:35:44.730 --> 00:35:45.870
Michael's real sour.

824
00:35:46.070 --> 00:35:46.890
He doesn't want to go.

825
00:35:47.110 --> 00:35:49.810
There was a series of talking heads here in the shooting draft,

826
00:35:49.810 --> 00:35:52.610
and several people share their thoughts on musical theater.

827
00:35:53.210 --> 00:35:53.730
Oh.

828
00:35:53.970 --> 00:35:54.210
Yeah.

829
00:35:54.210 --> 00:35:54.610
Yes.

830
00:35:54.810 --> 00:35:58.990
I really want to share with you what Stanley has to say and his feelings about standing ovations.

831
00:35:59.990 --> 00:36:04.090
I'll be there, but do not expect to stand an ovation.

832
00:36:04.730 --> 00:36:09.810
I am sick of going to shows and feeling like I need to stand when the show isn't that good.

833
00:36:10.270 --> 00:36:11.690
You hear me, Steve Harvey?

834
00:36:15.270 --> 00:36:17.050
Throwing shade at Steve Harvey?

835
00:36:18.270 --> 00:36:19.890
I love this talking head.

836
00:36:19.930 --> 00:36:20.670
I did, too.

837
00:36:21.330 --> 00:36:25.650
I've been to shows where everyone's standing up at the end.

838
00:36:25.790 --> 00:36:28.170
It's almost like we stand up at the end of every show.

839
00:36:28.330 --> 00:36:29.970
And then you feel bad if you don't.

840
00:36:30.290 --> 00:36:30.570
Yeah.

841
00:36:30.830 --> 00:36:31.910
And I had a little Stanley.

842
00:36:32.070 --> 00:36:35.570
I was like, well, how are they going to know when it's a real standing ovation?

843
00:36:36.450 --> 00:36:38.450
If we stand at the end of all of them?

844
00:36:38.570 --> 00:36:41.270
It's sort of like, you know, the participation prize.

845
00:36:41.670 --> 00:36:42.190
Mm-hmm.

846
00:36:42.510 --> 00:36:46.010
But then I know what it takes to put on a theater production.

847
00:36:46.290 --> 00:36:47.530
And you're like, you better stand.

848
00:36:47.570 --> 00:36:48.490
Just stand up.

849
00:36:48.530 --> 00:36:49.670
We worked our butts off.

850
00:36:49.770 --> 00:36:50.090
Yeah.

851
00:36:50.670 --> 00:36:56.010
Just getting a show off the ground and from beginning to end, stand on up.

852
00:36:56.390 --> 00:36:57.590
I feel both ways.

853
00:36:58.030 --> 00:37:00.250
This talking head really made me laugh.

854
00:37:00.410 --> 00:37:05.410
And then also now when you rewatch this episode, at the end when they give a standing ovation,

855
00:37:05.410 --> 00:37:11.350
it cuts to Stanley and he's got the biggest grump face because he has to now stand and he's

856
00:37:11.350 --> 00:37:12.030
really annoyed.

857
00:37:12.230 --> 00:37:12.710
You know what?

858
00:37:12.710 --> 00:37:13.210
It's funny.

859
00:37:13.390 --> 00:37:19.010
I know they cut the talking head, but everything about that shot of him like doing the obligatory

860
00:37:19.670 --> 00:37:21.850
standing ovation told me the whole story.

861
00:37:22.070 --> 00:37:22.170
Yep.

862
00:37:22.370 --> 00:37:24.070
I knew all his feelings about it.

863
00:37:24.070 --> 00:37:29.210
Well, lady, Dwight is not going to go because the last time he went to the theater, a man

864
00:37:29.210 --> 00:37:30.970
dressed as a cat sat on his lap.

865
00:37:30.970 --> 00:37:33.870
I feel like Angela made him go to cats.

866
00:37:34.270 --> 00:37:36.790
Lady, we got so much mail from people.

867
00:37:36.990 --> 00:37:37.350
You did?

868
00:37:38.030 --> 00:37:39.010
Kobe S.

869
00:37:39.190 --> 00:37:40.690
from Wisconsin said,

870
00:37:40.870 --> 00:37:45.670
in my mind, I have come to believe that Dwight attended a local production of cats to appease

871
00:37:45.670 --> 00:37:46.130
Angela.

872
00:37:46.370 --> 00:37:49.830
And I believe Andy was the cat who sat on Dwight's lap.

873
00:37:51.370 --> 00:37:57.290
Because in season five, employee transfer, Andy dressed like a cat from a production of cats.

874
00:37:57.550 --> 00:37:57.810
Yes.

875
00:37:58.410 --> 00:38:03.590
I was so curious about that line that I went to the shooting draft to see if it said something

876
00:38:03.590 --> 00:38:07.830
like Dwight glances over at Angela, Angela rolls her eyes or something like that.

877
00:38:07.830 --> 00:38:11.290
It didn't, but I fully believe that Angela made him go to cats.

878
00:38:11.590 --> 00:38:13.610
I'm with you and Kobe S.

879
00:38:13.790 --> 00:38:14.510
Yes, Kobe.

880
00:38:15.070 --> 00:38:16.590
Guess who wants to go to the play?

881
00:38:17.030 --> 00:38:17.650
I know.

882
00:38:18.030 --> 00:38:18.430
Angela.

883
00:38:18.430 --> 00:38:22.310
Angela, I think this is proving the point that she goes to musicals.

884
00:38:22.430 --> 00:38:24.990
And she says she would like Dwight to take her.

885
00:38:25.110 --> 00:38:27.370
And he's like, just disrobe.

886
00:38:27.490 --> 00:38:28.750
Let's get to it, basically.

887
00:38:28.970 --> 00:38:29.690
And she's like, uh-uh.

888
00:38:29.970 --> 00:38:30.530
No, no, no.

889
00:38:30.670 --> 00:38:32.950
You have to take me to the play to get the punch.

890
00:38:33.570 --> 00:38:35.530
I have a location alert for this scene.

891
00:38:36.090 --> 00:38:37.370
Yes, it's a new location.

892
00:38:37.970 --> 00:38:39.710
This is Dwight's office.

893
00:38:40.130 --> 00:38:44.370
Now that he's the owner of the building, this is his business manager office.

894
00:38:44.710 --> 00:38:47.790
Yes, and it's much nicer than his office at Dunder Mifflin.

895
00:38:47.950 --> 00:38:48.250
Yeah.

896
00:38:48.430 --> 00:38:49.110
That's true.

897
00:38:49.270 --> 00:38:49.950
He doesn't have.

898
00:38:50.070 --> 00:38:51.510
He's just part of a desk clump.

899
00:38:51.930 --> 00:38:57.630
Well, this was designed by Michael Gallenberg and built by Tim James, and it existed over on our warehouse set.

900
00:38:57.630 --> 00:39:05.870
At 4 minutes 28 seconds, did any of you guys notice the picture of the drone, the predator drone, big picture?

901
00:39:06.030 --> 00:39:06.470
It's tilted.

902
00:39:06.690 --> 00:39:08.110
Yes, I did notice that.

903
00:39:08.110 --> 00:39:09.690
I was wondering why it was tilted.

904
00:39:10.510 --> 00:39:12.210
I figured it had to be on purpose.

905
00:39:13.670 --> 00:39:16.810
I went to the shooting draft to see, and this is how it read.

906
00:39:16.810 --> 00:39:19.210
Dwight is in his building management office.

907
00:39:19.210 --> 00:39:22.450
Dwight is in his building management office hanging a picture of a predator drone, and Angela enters.

908
00:39:23.030 --> 00:39:24.850
Angela says, it's crooked.

909
00:39:25.270 --> 00:39:29.210
Dwight says, yes, and if I come back and it's straight, I'll know someone was here.

910
00:39:29.930 --> 00:39:31.690
Angela says, smart.

911
00:39:33.470 --> 00:39:38.850
And then she goes on to say, I want you to take me to the play this evening, and the scene continues as it aired.

912
00:39:39.190 --> 00:39:42.610
Well, Dwight does agree to take her to the play, but he's not wearing a cape.

913
00:39:42.850 --> 00:39:43.210
Okay.

914
00:39:44.250 --> 00:39:46.830
This made me realize, does he always wear a cape to plays?

915
00:39:47.070 --> 00:39:50.130
Like, he's not going to do his usual zhuzh for her.

916
00:39:50.370 --> 00:39:52.130
Yeah, has she asked in the past?

917
00:39:52.330 --> 00:39:53.450
And he wore a cape.

918
00:39:53.590 --> 00:39:54.510
Maybe to cats.

919
00:39:54.730 --> 00:39:55.030
Yes.

920
00:39:55.650 --> 00:40:01.610
Angela's going to have a talking head where she says, Dwight and I have a contractual agreement to procreate five times, plain and simple.

921
00:40:01.930 --> 00:40:09.470
And should he develop feelings for me, well, that would be permissible under item 7C, clause 2, so I would not object.

922
00:40:10.130 --> 00:40:13.910
So, that little smile at the end that I do, I improvised.

923
00:40:14.310 --> 00:40:14.710
Oh.

924
00:40:14.830 --> 00:40:18.590
It wasn't in the shooting draft, and they really liked it, and they asked me to do it every time.

925
00:40:18.890 --> 00:40:21.130
Oh, I love when we would do things like that.

926
00:40:21.290 --> 00:40:21.850
I know.

927
00:40:21.850 --> 00:40:25.590
It was fun to see it again, because I remembered, all of a sudden, all those memories came back.

928
00:40:25.610 --> 00:40:26.410
I was like, oh, yeah.

929
00:40:26.670 --> 00:40:31.810
We thought maybe she would give away a little bit of her true feelings, just for a second.

930
00:40:32.110 --> 00:40:34.610
It would always just fill me with such pride as an actor.

931
00:40:34.610 --> 00:40:43.430
If I was there, performing for the director and the writer, and I did something with something they'd written for me, and they asked me to do it over and over again.

932
00:40:43.430 --> 00:40:43.750
Yeah, yeah.

933
00:40:43.930 --> 00:40:44.690
I was like, oh, yeah.

934
00:40:44.770 --> 00:40:45.110
Oh, yeah.

935
00:40:45.210 --> 00:40:45.650
I did it.

936
00:40:45.710 --> 00:40:46.070
I did it.

937
00:40:46.130 --> 00:40:46.410
I love it.

938
00:40:46.890 --> 00:40:51.310
Pam and Jim are in a pickle, because they actually really want to go, but they don't have a babysitter.

939
00:40:52.130 --> 00:40:52.490
Yes.

940
00:40:52.770 --> 00:40:57.090
Andy comes in the kitchen and asks them if they could just keep looking.

941
00:40:57.370 --> 00:40:57.730
Mm-hmm.

942
00:40:57.730 --> 00:41:00.190
And Pam's like, yes, we will keep looking.

943
00:41:00.450 --> 00:41:03.030
There was a joint Jim and Pam talking head.

944
00:41:03.490 --> 00:41:04.010
Yes.

945
00:41:04.270 --> 00:41:06.890
About how much they love going to musical theater.

946
00:41:07.150 --> 00:41:07.750
I know.

947
00:41:08.010 --> 00:41:08.870
I know.

948
00:41:09.110 --> 00:41:16.270
When Randy was giving us his insider info on this episode, for this scene, he wrote, Jenna, you sound like you have a cold.

949
00:41:16.650 --> 00:41:17.450
Oh, yeah.

950
00:41:17.530 --> 00:41:17.990
I did.

951
00:41:18.250 --> 00:41:18.650
You did.

952
00:41:18.770 --> 00:41:19.570
I think I did have a cold.

953
00:41:19.750 --> 00:41:19.890
Yeah.

954
00:41:20.570 --> 00:41:22.170
Well, it's time for the show.

955
00:41:22.550 --> 00:41:24.710
The Loose Screw Playhouse is buzzing.

956
00:41:25.370 --> 00:41:27.590
Yes, everyone has made it.

957
00:41:27.590 --> 00:41:27.870
Yeah.

958
00:41:28.330 --> 00:41:30.610
Phyllis is wearing her fur stole.

959
00:41:30.850 --> 00:41:31.070
Yes.

960
00:41:31.070 --> 00:41:33.290
And she's arrived with Bob Vance.

961
00:41:33.430 --> 00:41:33.750
Mm-hmm.

962
00:41:34.310 --> 00:41:36.690
Stanley has brought his mistress.

963
00:41:37.110 --> 00:41:37.470
Cynthia.

964
00:41:37.730 --> 00:41:38.110
Mm-hmm.

965
00:41:38.490 --> 00:41:43.670
There was a whole runner where Phyllis thinks Cynthia's hitting on Bob, and she's ticked off.

966
00:41:43.790 --> 00:41:44.430
I loved it.

967
00:41:44.730 --> 00:41:45.290
I know.

968
00:41:45.350 --> 00:41:46.270
It's in deleted scenes.

969
00:41:46.310 --> 00:41:47.150
It's so good.

970
00:41:47.810 --> 00:41:50.050
We had a fan question from Aaron Y.

971
00:41:50.210 --> 00:41:51.330
in Springfield, Ohio.

972
00:41:51.650 --> 00:41:54.770
Where was the performance of Andy's play located?

973
00:41:55.430 --> 00:41:57.610
Well, this was a little bit of trickery.

974
00:41:57.850 --> 00:41:58.330
Hmm.

975
00:41:58.710 --> 00:42:07.650
The exterior shot of the theater that you just see very briefly with no people in front of it, that was the Matcha Theater in West Hollywood.

976
00:42:07.650 --> 00:42:14.490
We used an old stock photo of the theater and added graphics on top to say Loose Screw Playhouse.

977
00:42:14.490 --> 00:42:22.410
But the whole interior, the lobby, backstage, the stage, that was the Assistance League Theater.

978
00:42:22.410 --> 00:42:28.470
We shot there for four days, and our base camp was in a Sears parking lot that was nearby.

979
00:42:28.830 --> 00:42:29.050
Hmm.

980
00:42:30.050 --> 00:42:32.510
Lady, I feel like there's a chance you went to Sears one day.

981
00:42:32.990 --> 00:42:34.090
Maybe at lunch.

982
00:42:34.250 --> 00:42:35.230
Sounds like you.

983
00:42:35.390 --> 00:42:36.310
I'm sure I did.

984
00:42:36.570 --> 00:42:36.930
Oh!

985
00:42:37.070 --> 00:42:37.930
I went there!

986
00:42:38.190 --> 00:42:42.670
I like that you just had a memory of yourself going.

987
00:42:44.290 --> 00:42:46.050
And you got so excited.

988
00:42:46.410 --> 00:42:47.650
I remember this!

989
00:42:47.790 --> 00:42:48.610
I was so excited.

990
00:42:48.710 --> 00:42:52.230
You know, it's not easy to find a Sears in Los Angeles, California.

991
00:42:52.230 --> 00:42:52.590
No, it's not.

992
00:42:52.610 --> 00:42:55.350
It's where I bought my first washer and dryer when I moved here.

993
00:42:55.470 --> 00:42:59.530
Yeah, and I had to go far into the valley to find a Sears.

994
00:42:59.730 --> 00:42:59.910
Yeah.

995
00:43:00.470 --> 00:43:03.550
My very first credit card was a Sears credit card.

996
00:43:04.350 --> 00:43:04.810
Really?

997
00:43:04.970 --> 00:43:06.930
That I applied for by myself.

998
00:43:07.270 --> 00:43:08.570
I was so excited.

999
00:43:08.570 --> 00:43:09.890
I had a credit card.

1000
00:43:10.390 --> 00:43:11.690
But only for Sears.

1001
00:43:11.690 --> 00:43:12.630
But only for Sears.

1002
00:43:13.310 --> 00:43:13.830
Wow.

1003
00:43:14.170 --> 00:43:15.910
My first pair of roller skates!

1004
00:43:15.910 --> 00:43:19.050
I ordered out of a catalog because I'm 100 years old.

1005
00:43:19.190 --> 00:43:21.010
We were living in Jakarta, Indonesia.

1006
00:43:21.310 --> 00:43:24.330
We got the Sears catalog, and I ordered a pair of roller skates.

1007
00:43:24.470 --> 00:43:30.030
And eight months later, they were mine because it took forever to get anything overseas at that time.

1008
00:43:30.170 --> 00:43:34.770
Well, listen, I don't know what Sears is like today, but back in ye olden days.

1009
00:43:34.830 --> 00:43:35.890
Ye olden times!

1010
00:43:36.210 --> 00:43:41.410
That's where you bought all of your appliances because they had wonderful warranties and great service.

1011
00:43:41.690 --> 00:43:41.910
Yeah!

1012
00:43:42.590 --> 00:43:42.870
Mm-hmm.

1013
00:43:43.690 --> 00:43:52.430
Michael is gonna arrive, and one of my favorite lines that teases up the best tag of any episode, I think,

1014
00:43:53.170 --> 00:43:54.730
is an usher says,

1015
00:43:54.810 --> 00:43:58.850
Hey, are you the guy that did the entire Law & Order episode for your audition?

